## arithmetic on the doc's own layout
  as DOCUMENTED  64+128+128+32+24+4+4+4 = 388 but the line asserts 384 -> off by 4
  as CODED       64+128+128+32+20+4+4+4 = 384 and the line asserts 384 -> CONSISTENT

## consequences, all three measured in the source
  1. Follow the DOC and the offsets reach 388 in a 384-byte buffer.
     The authoritative spec, if obeyed, breaks the type. The code is
     right; the specification is wrong; and nothing says so.
  2. write_str() truncates with min(s,max) and returns no error and no
     flag, so tags is silently lossy at 20 bytes. A field that can drop
     data without saying so is not a field, it is a hope.
  3. The doctest asserts_eq!(bytes.len(), 384) — and bytes is a
     [u8; BINARY_SIZE] with BINARY_SIZE = 384. It is 384 for ANY input
     under ANY layout including a wrong one. THE TEST CANNOT FAIL.
     This is the 6.8x constant's exact shape: a check that passes at any
     value whatsoever.

## verdict
  A type whose spec cannot add up its own fields cannot arbitrate a
  field width, so it cannot be the 64-Bit Quilt Word. This is what the
  48-invariant programme in types-REGISTRY.md exists to fix, and the
  lattice should treat the word as UNSPECIFIED until it carries a
  stated invariant AND a relation that catches a wrong field width.
